readings.c1s3:
{{smaller block|Bunch had put his boys to a famous school, where they might ''whop'' the French boys, and learn all the modern languages. {{right|''Thackeray'', Philip, xviii.}}}}

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' '''1.''' To vanish suddenly. ''Halliwell.'' [North. Eng.] —'''2.''' To plump suddenly down, as on the ground; flop; turn suddenly: as, she ''whopped'' down on the floor; the fish ''whopped'' over. [U. S.]

'''whop, whap''' (hwop), ''n.'' [< ME. ''whapp''; < ''whop'', ''v.'' Cf. ''quop''<sup>1</sup>, ''quap''<sup>1</sup>, and ''wap''<sup>1</sup>.] A heavy blow. [Colloq.]
{{smaller block|<poem>For a ''whapp'' so he whyned and whesid,
And ȝitt no lasshe to the lurdan was lente.</poem>{{right|''York Plays'', p. 326.}}}}

'''whopper, whapper''' (hwop′ėr), ''n.'' [< ''whop'', ''whap'', + ''-er''<sup>1</sup>. Cf. ''wapper''.] '''1.''' One who whops. —'''2.''' Anything uncommonly large: applied particularly to a monstrous lie. [Colloq.]
{{smaller block|This is a ''whopper'' that ’s after us. {{right|''Marryat'', Frank Mildmay, xx. (''Davies''.)}}
But he hardly deserves mercy, having told ''whoppers''. {{right|''Harper’s Mag.'', LXXII. 213.}}}}

'''whopping, whapping''' (hwop′ing), ''a.'' [Ppr. of ''whop'', ''v.'' Cf. ''wapping''.] Very large; thumping: as, a ''whopping'' big trout. [Colloq.]

'''whore''' (hōr), ''n.'' [With unorig. ''w'', as in ''whole'', etc.; < ME. ''hore'', a harlot (not in AS.), < Icel. ''hōra'', adulteress, = Sw. ''hora'' = Dan. ''hore'' = D. ''hoer'' = OHG. ''huora'', ''huorra'', MHG. ''huore'', G. ''hure'' (Goth. ''hōr'', f., not found, another word, ''kalki'', being used); also in masc. form, Icel. ''hōrr'' = Goth. ''hōrs'', adulterer; cf. AS. *''hōr'', adultery (in comp. ''hōrcwēn'', adulteress), < Icel. ''hōr'' = Sw. Dan. ''hor'' = OHG. ''huor'', adultery; cf. MHG.

readings.c2s2:
who prostitutes her body for hire; a prostitute; a harlot; a courtezan; a strumpet; hence, in abuse, any unchaste woman; an adulteress or fornicatress. [Now only in low use.]
{{smaller block|Do not marry me to a ''whore.'' ''Shak.'', M. for M., v. 1. 521.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Hee wooed her and sued her his mistress to bee,
And offered rich presents to Mary Ambree. . . .
“A mayden of England, sir, never will bee
The ''whore'' of a monarcke,” quoth Mary Ambree.</poem>
{{right|''Mary Ambree'' (Child’s Ballads, VII. 113).}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Thou know’st my Wrongs, and with what pain I wear
The Name of ''Whore'' his Preachment on me pinn’d.</poem>
{{right|''J. Beaumont'', Psyche, iii. 184.}}}}

'''whore''' (hōr), ''v.''; pret. and pp. ''whored'', ppr. ''whoring.'' [= G. ''huren'' = Sw. ''hora'' = Dan. ''hore''; cf. D. ''hoereren''; from the noun.] '''I.''' ''intrans.'' To prostitute one’s body for hire; in general, to practise lewdness. ''Shak.'', Othello, v. 1. 116. [Low.]

'''II.''' ''trans.'' To corrupt by lewd intercourse. [Low.]
{{smaller block|He that hath kill’d my king and ''whored'' my mother.
{{right|''Shak.'', Hamlet, v. 2. 64.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>A Vestal ravish’d, or a Matron ''whor’d'',
Are laudable Diversions in a Lord.</poem>
{{right|''Congreve'', tr. of Eleventh Satire of Juvenal.}}}}

'''whoredom''' (hōr′dum), ''n.'' [< ME. ''horedom'', ''hordom'', < Icel. ''hōrdōmr'' = Sw. ''hordom'' = OD. ''hoerdom'', whoredom; as ''whore'' + ''-dom''.] Prostitution of the body for hire; in general, the practice of unlawful sexual commerce. {{smaller|In Scripture the term is sometimes applied metaphorically to idolatry—the desertion of the worship of the true God for the worship of idols.}}
{{smaller block|Tamar . . . is with child by ''whoredom.'' Gen. xxxviii. 24.}}

readings.c3s1:
¶
'''whore’s-bird†''' (hōrz′bėrd), ''n.'' A low term of abuse.

{{smaller block|They’d set some sturdy ''whore’s-bird'' to meet me, and beat out ha’f a dozen of my teeth.
{{right|''Plautus made English'' (1694), p. 9. (''Davies.'')}}}}

{{smaller block|Damn you altogether for a pack of ''whores’-birds'' as you are. {{right|''Graves'', Spiritual Quixote, iv. 9. (''Davies.'')}}}}

'''whore’s-egg''' (hōrz′eg), ''n.'' A sea-urchin.

'''whoreson†''' (hōr′sun), ''n.'' and ''a.'' [Early mod. E. also ''horeson'', ''horson''; < ''whore'' + ''son''.] '''I.''' ''n.'' A bastard: used generally in contempt, or in coarse familiarity, and without exactness of meaning. [Low.]

{{smaller block|<poem>Well said; a merry ''whoreson'', ha!</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', R. and J., iv. 4. 19.}}}}

{{smaller block|Frog was a sly ''whoreson'', the reverse of John.
{{right|''Arbuthnot'', Hist. John Bull.}}}}

'''II.''' ''a.'' Bastard-like; mean; scurvy: used in contempt, or in coarse familiarity, and applied to persons or things.

{{smaller block|A ''whoreson'' cold, sir, a cough, sir.
{{right|''Shak.'', 2 Hen. IV., iii. 2. 193.}}}}

{{smaller block|The ''whoreson'' rich innkeeper of Doncaster, her father, shewed himself a rank ostler to send her up at this time a year, and by the carrier too.
{{right|''Dekker and Webster'', Northward Ho, ii. 2.}}}}

'''whorish''' (hōr′ish), ''a.'' [< ''whore'' + ''-ish''<sup>1</sup>.] Of or pertaining to whores; having the character of a whore; lewd; unchaste. ''Shak.'', T. and C., iv. 1. 63. [Low.]

{{smaller block|Your ''whorish'' love, your drunken healths, your houts and shouts. {{right|''Marston'', Antonio and Mellida, I., iv. 1.}}}}

readings.c3s2:
¶
'''whorishly''' (hōr′ish-li), ''adv.'' In a whorish or lewd manner. [Low.]

'''whorishness''' (hōr′ish-nes), ''n.'' The character of being whorish. [Low.]

'''whorl''' (hwėrl or hwôrl), ''n.'' [< late ME. ''whorle'', contr. of *''whorvel'', ''whorwhil'', ''whorwil''; cf. OD. ''worvel'', a spindle, whirl, etc.: see ''whirl'', and cf. ''wharl''<sup>1</sup>.] '''1.''' In ''bot.'', a ring of organs all from the same node; a verticil. {{smaller|Every complete flower is externally formed of two whorls of leaves, constituting the floral envelop, or perianth; and internally of two or more other whorls of organs, constituting the organs of fructification. The term ''whorl'' by itself is generally applied to a circle of radiating leaves—an arrangement of more than two leaves around a common center, upon the same plane with one another. Also ''whirl.'' See cuts under ''Lavandula'', ''Paris'', and ''Veronica.''}}

'''2.''' In ''conch.'', one of the turns of a spiral shell; a volution; a gyre. {{smaller|The last whorl, opposite the apex or nucleus, and including the aperture of the shell, is commonly distinguished as the ''body-whorl.'' See ''spire''<sup>2</sup>, ''n.'', 2 (with cut), and cuts under ''univalve'', ''Pleurotomaria'', and ''Scalaria.'' Also ''whirl.''}}
{{smaller block|<poem>See what a lovely shell, . . .
Made so fairily well,
With delicate spire and ''whorl.''</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', Maud, xxiv. 1.}}}}

'''3.''' In ''anat.'': (''a'') A volution or turn of the spiral cochlea of man or any mammal. See cut under ''ear.'' (''b'') A scroll
[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{smaller|Whorls of ''Ammonites rothomagensis.''}}}}
or turn of a turbinate bone, as the ethmoturbinal or maxilloturbinal. See cut under ''nasal.'' —'''4.''' The fly of a spindle, generally made of wood, sometimes of hard stone, etc. Also

